  "body": "relating to https://github.com/whatwg/streams/issues/20\n\nThere is a key distinction in duplex streams,\nThe simple case is a transform stream, which is like a meat grinder, because it takes an input and transforms it in someway, but is pretty much stateless\n(it may have configuration settings, like gzip or encryption, but the output is pretty much determined by the input)\n\nTransform streams represent probably 95% of the use of custom streams.\ntransform streams tend to handle back pressure very simply, passing any back-pressure signals from the readable side to the writable side (when the sausage is getting full, stop feeding in meat)\n\nOn the other hand, you can also have a duplex stream that is more like a telephone. Say you call up a database service via an rpc protocol,\nhere you GET and PUT data, and the output of the stream is determined not just by the input but also by the internal state of the database.\n\nThis second type of stream doesn't necessarily have the same relationship between readable and writable side back-pressure.\n\nIt's highly recommended to have a simple way of creating ad hoc transform streams, where back pressure signals are passed through for you.\n\nIn the other hand, duplex streams are usually protocols that are painstakingly written. As long as there is some way to create a duplex stream, and manage the back pressure signals manually then that is fine.\n",
